Archaeological excavations in 1932 at Samaria 
brought to light remains of buildings constructed by 
Kings Omri and Ahab and their successors. On the sum- 
mit bounded by steep slopes on three sides, Omri built a 
rectangular citadel, protected by a wall of square stones 
laid with great skill on levelled beds in the rock. The 
base courses of sections of this wall are well preserved. 
There are also remains of the lower city which was built 
at the same time on the more gradual southem slope of 
the site. In Ahab’s time, or possibly some years later, a 
city wall of great thickness was thrown up, and sections 
of this may also be seen today. 

Among the finds in the citadel were the carved ivories 
from Ahab’s ‘ivory house’, some bearing inscriptions in 
ancient Hebrew script; and scores of ostraca with Heb- 
rew writing discovered in the ruins of what was prob- 
ably the royal records office. The large well-plastered 
reservoir found by the archaeologists may have been 
the ‘pool of Samaria’ in which was ‘washed the chariot’ 
of Ahab after his death on the battlefield at Ramoth- 
gilead. (1 Kgs. 22:38) 

