AMOS (Heb. ‘burden’) c. 775-50 Bc. First of the 
literary prophets. 

Amos lived in Tekoa, a village in the Judean hills 
about six miles south of Bethlehem. Uzziah ruled 
at the time in the southern kingdom of Judah and 
Jeroboam 11 in the northern kingdom of Israel. Amos 
was a sheep-farmer, and also gathered the fruit of the 
sycamore tree (a kind of fig). His first appearance as a 
prophet was at a festival in the town of Bethel, in the 
kingdom of Israel. His opening words thundered a grim 


