BATHSHEBA (Heb. ‘daughter of the oath’) c. 10 
century Bc. Wife of David and mother of Solomon. 
One warm moonlight night early in David's reign, he 
was unable to sleep and paced restlessly along the roof- 
top of his palace in Jerusalem. Glancing down over the 
parapet, he was startled to see a beautiful young woman 
bathing herself on a nearby roof. On making enquiries 
he learnt that she was the daughter of Eliam and the 
granddaughter of his chief counsellor, Ahithophel ; and 
that she was married to Uriah, a Hittite officer serving 
under Joab in the siege of Rabbah, the capital of 
Ammon. Yielding to sudden temptation, David sent 


