Pictorial Images and Oracles ] 
Ezekiel’s love of visual imagery expressed itself at times 
in ‘acting out’ his message. This use of mime may have 
been connected with his disclosure that at times the 
Lord made him dumb. 

For instance, the prophet describes the elaborate en- 
actment of the siege and fall of Jerusalem, before the 
event took place. He was instructed to draw a map of 
the city on a clay tablet, place it on the ground, and 
draw round it furrows and other indications of siege. 
Playing the role of God, he flung out his bare arm and 
condemned the city. After that he lay for some time on 
his left side and then on his right side, to indicate the 

















