supply of Rabbah. He proposed that David himself 
move up with fresh troops for the final assault ‘lest I 
take the city, and it be called by my name’ (2 Sam. 
12:28). In this way Joab demonstrated that his king’s 
renown mattered more than his own. 

Joab and David’s Sons 

Three years after David’s favourtte son Absalom mur- 
dered his elder brother Amnon and fled into exile, it 
was Joab who tried to bring about a reconciliation. He 
did not presume to broach so sensitive a subject 
directly. Instead he brought before the king a woman 
from the town of Tekoah who pretended one of her 
sons had killed the other. When David’s compassion 
had been aroused she referred to his own banished son. 
The king relented and sent Joab to fetch Absalom back 
to Jerusalem. 

But the conflict between father and son had not been 
resolved. Absalom launched a rebellion, and Joab was 
one of the loyal group that fled with David. In the 
crucial battle that later took place in the woods of 
Ephraim, east of the Jordan river, David’s forces under 
Joab’s command defeated those of Absalom. David 
had instructed his commanders that no harm should 
come to his rebellious son but it was not in Joab’s 
nature to spare even a kinsman who had risen against 


