The author of the Book of Job was one of the boldest 
and most original religious thinkers and literary masters 
ever to have existed. He was as familiar with the intel- 
lectual heritage of his time as with the life of the desert, 
and he did not hesitate to challenge and probe accepted 
beliefs. The identity of the man who penned Job’s im- 
mortal dialogue with God and his fellow-men remains 
one of the unresolved mysteries of the Old Testament. 
