made. Joshua sent out a team of three persons from 
each tribe to survey the territory not yet allocated. They 
were to ‘describe the land in seven divisions and bring 
the description here to me.’ (Josh. 18:6) On the basis 
of their report tribal areas were marked out, and lots 
drawn for them at a gathering of the people called by 
Joshua at the sanctuary at Shiloh. Benjamin’s portion 
was a narrow belt of hill-country just north of Jeru- 
salem. Dan was given a strip of foothills and coastal 
plain to the west of Benjamin. Simeon remained in the 
northern Negev round Beersheba. The north was divi- 
ded between Asher (western Galilee), Naphtali and 
Zebulun (eastern and central Galilee), and Issachar 
(the eastern Jezreel and the Beth-shean valley). (See 
