memorial in Jerusalem.’ (Neh. 2:20) Sanballat con- 
tinued to mock them, and asked whether these ‘feeble 
Jews’ really wanted to ‘revive the stones out of 
the heaps of rubbish, and burned ones at that?’ (Neh. 
4:2) Tobiah added that even a fox would be able to 
break down their stone wall. 

But when the wall reached mid-height and the 
builders were getting tired and discouraged, their 
opponents threatened to stop them by force. Nehemiah 
made security arrangements for the work to continue. 
He posted an armed militia day and night, and also 
made each of the workers carry a weapon. ‘And each 
of the builders had his sword girded at his side while he 
built.’ (Neh. 4:18) Since the working parties were strung 
out in different sectors at some distance from each 
other, he commanded that they should all rally when 
they heard the trumpet sound. At night they remained 
within the walls. Nehemiah records that during this 
period neither he nor the members of his family nor his 
bodyguard took off their clothes except for washing. 

These vigorous precautions had their effect, and the 
idea of armed intervention was dropped. Instead, his 
enemies resorted to various intrigues. They invited 
Nehemiah to meet them for a parley, but he distrusted 
them and declined. Sanballat then sent Nehemiah a 


