became part of the Persian empire. The following year, 
in 538 Bc, Cyrus issued an edict permitting the Judean 
exiles to return to their homeland if they wished, and to 
rebuild the Temple. Those who remained behind were 
to assist the repatriates with money and goods, and to 
give them voluntary donations for the Temple. A 
total of 42,360 Jews gathered together for the return, 
under the leadership of Sheshbazzar, also of the 
Judean royal dynasty. In listing the other prominent 
persons going on the journey, Zerubbabel’s name is the 
first one mentioned. (Some scholars believe that he and 
Sheshbazzar were the same person.) When everything 
was ready, the caravan moved off slowly on the six- 
hundred-mile trek across the desert. 


