isthmus to Corinth. 

Three hundred years previously, Athens had been the 
intellectual centre of the world. The Athenians, there- 
fore, were hardly likely to be impressed by the insignifi- 
cant figure and rough-and-ready oratory of the Jew 
from Tarsus who impertinently proclaimed: ‘Men of 
Athens, I perceive that in every way you are very relig- 
