The whole building was elaborately decorated with 
carved stone ornaments. Its walls may have been 
covered with frescoes, like those of the nearly con- 
temporary Dura synagogue, built in aD 244. As 
Professor Albright has pointed out, the catacombs in 
Rome and the necropolis at Beth She’arim demonstrate 
the dependence of early Chmistian art on Jewish 
frescoes of the Roman period. What is really striking at 
Capernaum is the variety of the motifs, particularly the 
mixture of Jewish and Roman symbols. Among the 
