Paradoxically, the earliest traditional and archaeolo- 
gical evidence, probably 4th-century Byzantine as well 
as Crusader, supports a location at Amwas (Arabic for 
Emmaus), one hundred and sixty stadia from Jerusalem, 
as quoted in Codex Sinaiticus, which was known to 
both Bishop Eusebius of Caesarea and to Jerome, who 
translated the Vulgate. Over the hills from Jerusalem, 
however, Amwas is only 15 miles. Therefore, this site is 
within reach of the possibility of the double journey 
from and back to Jerusalem in an afternoon. 

