Here, Paul spent the first three months of his ministry 
teaching in the synagogue. When the final break with 
the Jewish community occurred, he moved to the 
lecture-room of Tyrannus. There he worked daily in 
teaching and discussion among Jews and Greeks, over 
a period of two whole years, in the siesta hours of noon 
to four in the afternoon. Remarkable progress followed 
this systematic instruction, accompanied also by a 
ministry of healing. Both Jews and Greeks came to 
respect the name of Jesus, rather than the magical arts 
of Diana. Paul despatched teachers to Colossae and 
Macedonia; at this time also the Seven Churches of 
Asia, mentioned by John in the Book of Revelation, 
were founded. (see JOHN 9.) Paul, when he closed 
his ministry in Ephesus, travelled overland to Troas, 


