described as the Magna Carta of Christian freedom. 
His plea for freedom from legalistic codes presents 
timeless Christian principles. Paul’s attack on the 
influence of extraneous religious practices on the belief 
in God, as revealed through Jesus, is nowhere so clearly 
stated as in this letter. He singled out the danger of the 
rigid observance of external rules as a fundamental 
stumbling-block of his day; and 1t is still a relevant issue 
in the Christian Church today. 

There were two Galatias in New Testament times: 
the old kingdom of Galatia along the south coast of the 
Black Sea, and the Roman province of Galatia, an 
enlargement of the kingdom and bordering the Mediter- 
ranean to include those towns visited by Paul and 
Barnabas on the First Journey. Paul’s letter was in- 
tended-either for the Christian Churches of the Roman 
province at Iconium, Lystra, and Derbe, or for other 


