This letter was written round about the year 80, as a 
warming against false teachers in the Church. The writer 
calls himself ‘Jude, a servant of Jesus Christ, and 
brother of James’. James, leader of the Church at 
Jerusalem for some twenty years, was obviously known 
to his readers. There seems little reason to doubt the 
author’s claim to his relationship. The destination of the 
letter cannot be determined, but it appears to have been 
somewhere where the interpretation of the gospel and 
Christian life was far too free and undisciplined. The 
writer had to encourage his readers to ‘contend for the 
faith which was once for all delivered to the saints. For 
admission has been secretly gained by some who long 
ago were designated for this condemnation, ungodly 
persons who pervert the grace of our God into licen- 
