Night and day among the tombs and on the mountains 
he was always crying out, and bruising himself with 
stones. And when he saw Jesus from afar, he ran and 
worshipped him; and crying out with a loud voice, he 
said, ““What have you to do with me, Jesus, Son of the 
Most High God? I adjure you by God, do not torment 
me.” For he had said to him, “Come out of the man, 
you unclean spirit!” And Jesus asked him, “What is 
your name?” He replied, ““My name is Legion; for we 
are many.”” (Mark 5:4-9) 

In primitive communities, mental illness is naturally 
interpreted as possession by evil spirits who need to be 
tamed by force. The use of chains for this purpose was 
common in medieval Europe to restrain the evil in the 
person, which appeared to reduce him to the level of a 
savage animal. In the primitive thinking of | st-century 
Palestine, the power to suppress evil spirits could only 
come from God, the all-good. Therefore anyone able to 
exercise such power over evil received his power from 
God, and the miracles of Jesus were consequently 


