Lydia 


the little River Ganga, outside Philippi, a Roman 
colony and city founded by Alexander the Great in 
Macedonia. Lydia’s household was the first in Europe 
known to have been converted and baptized. 

Paul found few Jews at Philippi and no synagogue. 
He probably waited some days until the Sabbath and 
then went to the river, where he might expect any local 
Jews to be at prayer. There, he met a few women who 
gathered regularly for prayer; among these was Lydia, 
who was in the purple-dye trade and came from the 
city of Thyatira, 100 miles or more north of Ephesus in 
Asia Minor. Paul and his companions sat down and 
talked to the women who had gathered; Lydia listened 
and opened her heart to what Paul said; she was herself 
a pagan and only loosely attached to the little Jewish 
community. She insisted on being baptized by Paul, 
together with her household, and begged the three 
visitors, Paul, Timothy, and Silas, to accept hospitality. 
‘If you have judged me to be faithful to the Lord, come 
to my house and stay.’ 





