By the time of Jesus, the Scribes had developed a 
complicated system of traditions to ensure the correct 
application of the Law. They had come to demand and 
to receive the deep respect of the people. The very title 
‘Rabbi’, meaning ‘My Teacher’, by which some were 
known indicates the honour in which they were held. 
In the Gospels they are often linked with the Pharisees, 
whose convictions some Scribes shared as Pharisees. 
There were, however, Scribes within the Sanhedrin, the 
supreme Council, both Pharisaic and apparently Sad- 
ducean Scribes. These are mentioned among the mem- 
bers of the court which convicted Jesus of blasphemy in 
Jerusalem. 

In Galilee, too, the Scribes were highly critical of 
Jesus’s activities, particularly at Capernaum, where the 
synagogue congregation compared Jesus’s authorita- 
tive preaching with the less inspired efforts of their 
Scribes. The Scribes objected strongly to Jesus’s words 
of forgiveness to the paralytic, saying, “Who can for- 
give sins but God alone?’ Again, they complained at 
his eating with Matthew the publican and his colleagues. 
In the Gospel of Luke, Jesus replies to them with the 
parables of the lost and the found: the Lost Sheep, the 





